<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="preface">Preface</h2>

  <p><em>Please, Don't Put It Down. It Will Get Worse</em></p>

  <p>For 17 years running, as often as time would permit and mental gears would mesh, I wrote a column entitled "Once Around The Thundermug" for a weekly publication. A chubby little dough mixer around my house and other well intentioned persons pressured me into writing three small booklets employing the Thundermug moniker.</p>

  <p>I was scared and embarrassed in presenting the first column to the newspaper. I still harbor that fear as I prepare, for a fourth time, to expose my ignorance over a broader area than the newspaper afforded. Fame is fleeting but failure is forever. I would like a brief fling with fame but I have begun to feel comfortable with failure and a change in this late stage of my life might prove disastrous.</p>

  <p>In the first publications I struggled to stay within the guidelines of literature and real writers. I spoke of this with a friend so he could have some concept of the work involved and might offer some praise. "Oh horseshit," he exclaimed, "nobody reads that crap in the front of a book and you know it...you fan the pages to the first chapter and if a paragraph grabs you you might finish reading it...I don't look to see who the author is unless I really like the book...if you don't know the author personally it doesn't mean a damned thing."</p>

  <p>That conversation was a little deflating but it is the truth.</p>

  <p>We are going to change the format on this one in respect of my friend and his advice. We will number the pages for convenience and this page will be the only introduction to whatever follows. "All that crap" as he so aptly described it will be on the final two or three pages if included at all.</p>

  <p>I have lived a good deal longer than I expected or deserved in the lifestyle I was destined for and it has been a very rewarding journey. My greatest disappointment is to have known so many wonderful persons who have drifted into obscurity and are only remembered when we feel duty bound to tidy up a grave on Memorial Day. Mining and miners have taken a bum rap over the past 20 years. We must remember that the metals refined from the crude ore cradled in Mother Earth helped make a better life for everyone in our country and were a vital contribution in winning two wars for freedom.</p>

  <p>Mining was an industry that afforded those with meager or interrupted educations to compete. I received a note from a cheerful little guy working two levels down from my drift. It read "pleez sen chuk ranch to 7 levul." Pat had little formal education but he was a top miner and when television came to Creede he taught himself the basics of video reception, enough expertise to enable him to repair sets and help people bring the medium to their homes.</p>

  <p>If you recognize a name in one of the stories take a moment to remember their contribution to our well being. I will appreciate that.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page iii. "Chuk" and "levul" are printed as shown, part of the quoted note.</em></p>
</section>

