  <h2 id="from-the-barrel-of-common-sense">From the Barrel of Common Sense</h2>

  <p>"I ain't never had much but I guess I never wanted much except a warm house and good friends."&#8212;George Horne</p>

  <p>"Never work with a man who wears gloves. He will spend half his time either pulling them on, taking them off or looking for them."&#8212;Al Turnipseed</p>

  <p>"There are a lot of people I have respected in my life. I try to remember the qualities they had that made me respect them. It sort of gives you a guideline for the way you treat people."&#8212;Erick Nelson</p>

  <p>"If your job interferes with your fishing you should look for a new job. If your job and your wife interfere with your fishing you'd be better off to give her your job and go fishing."&#8212;Horace Wheeler</p>

  <p>"It's easier to fire a man if you're paying him more than he's worth."&#8212;Art Fairchild...explaining why Creede Transportation Company paid $5.00 per shift when the mining companies were paying $4.00 per shift.</p>

  <p>"Tighten it just as tight as you can then give it one more turn."&#8212;Isaac D. Crawford on installing pipeline in a mine.</p>

  <p>"Load the rear of the shovel and the front will take care of itself."&#8212;Kearney Higgs</p>

  <p>"You can forgive a man for just about anything if he's honest."&#8212;Allen Hosselkus</p>

  <p>"The good Lord never made anyone perfect but he could have tried a little harder with Les Turnipseed and John Jackson."&#8212;Mary E. Schriver</p>

  <p>"There is no sound on Earth as pleasing to the ear as the laughter of children."&#8212;Josephine Jackson</p>

  <p>"Respect is the most important and valuable possession any person can strive for, it is the ultimate compliment to tell a person that you respect him or her."&#8212;Dean Hammond</p>

  <p>"A hundred years from now we'll see this in a different light."&#8212;Charles Steele</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page iv. Twelve people are named on this page as sources of these remarks, and Les Turnipseed is named within Mary E. Schriver's; each is indexed separately under this page's URL rather than under a specific story.</em></p>
